/* DEEP NOTES part 3 — A Level all subjects */
window.EV_HUB_DEEP = window.EV_HUB_DEEP || {};
Object.assign(window.EV_HUB_DEEP, {
"alevel/m9709":[
{t:"1. Pure 1 toolkit",b:"<ul><li>Quadratics: discriminant conditions, completing the square for vertex form, roots relations S = −b/a, P = c/a; solve inequalities with the parabola's shape (sketch first).</li><li>Functions: domain/range, composite fg (inside first), inverse f⁻¹, transformations y = f(x)+a, y = f(x+a), y = af(x), y = f(ax) — know each's effect cold.</li><li>Coordinate geometry: line equations, parallel/perpendicular conditions, midpoints, distances; circle (x−a)² + (y−b)² = r² and the expanded form.</li><li>Circular measure: radians (π rad = 180°), s = rθ, A = ½r²θ — sectors, segments, arc problems.</li><li>Trigonometry: identities (sin²+cos² = 1, tan = sin/cos), solving equations with general solutions (nπ ± α for sin, 2nπ ± α for cos, nπ + α for tan); R-formula a cosθ + b sinθ = R cos(θ − α).</li><li>Differentiation from first principles; chain/product/quotient; stationary points classified; integration as reverse; definite integrals = areas (areas BELOW axis give negative — split!); volumes of revolution.</li></ul>"},
{t:"2. Pure 2 & 3 extensions",b:"<ul><li>Algebra: modulus |x|, solving |f(x)| = g(x) by squaring or case split; polynomials — factor/remainder theorem, solving cubics; partial fractions (distinct, repeated, quadratic factors).</li><li>Logs & exponentials: ln x and e^x, solving a^x = b via logs, growth/decay models (population, cooling, compound interest continuous).</li><li>Trigonometry: sec/cosec/cot, identities 1 + tan² = sec², solving harder equations in given ranges; harmonic form for max/min problems.</li><li>Differentiation: implicit, parametric (dy/dx = (dy/dt)/(dx/dt)), d²y/dx² for parametric (divide by dx/dt again); connected rates of change.</li><li>Integration: substitution (choose u = inner function), parts (∫u dv = uv − ∫v du, LIATE), improper integrals with limits; differential equations — separate variables then apply initial conditions; exponential growth solutions N = N₀e^(kt).</li><li>Vectors: 3D lines r = a + λb; shortest distance from point to line; intersection of lines; scalar product for angles (cos θ = a·b/|a||b|) and perpendicularity.</li></ul>"},
{t:"3. Mechanics (P4)",b:"<ul><li>Forces & equilibrium: resolving, triangle of forces, friction F ≤ μR (limiting at equality), inclined planes (split weight into mg sinθ ∥, mg cosθ ⊥).</li><li>Kinematics: SUVAT extended with calculus (v = ds/dt, a = dv/dt, s = ∫v dt); motion graphs; multi-stage journeys.</li><li>Newton's 2nd law: F = ma as a vector equation; connected particles (pulleys, lifts — apparent weight), momentum & impulse, conservation of momentum in 1D collisions (direct impact, Newton's law of restitution e = speed of separation/speed of approach for A2 extension).</li><li>Work, energy, power: work–energy theorem, GPE/KE exchange with friction losses; power P = Fv (max speed when P = resistance × v — the car-top-speed classic).</li></ul>"},
{t:"4. Probability & Statistics (P5)",b:"<ul><li>Permutations & combinations: nPr, nCr; arrangements with repeats n!/(a!b!); selection problems (at least one = complement).</li><li>Probability: conditional P(A|B) = P(A∩B)/P(B); independence test P(A∩B) = P(A)P(B); tree & Venn methods.</li><li>Discrete random variables: E(X) = Σxp, Var(X) = E(X²) − [E(X)]²; Binomial B(n,p): P(X=r) = C(n,r)p^r q^(n−r), mean np, variance npq; Geometric distribution.</li><li>Continuous RV: pdf f(x) — total area 1, P(a<X<b) = ∫f dx, E(X) = ∫xf dx, median from F(x) = 0.5; Uniform distribution.</li><li>Normal distribution: z = (X−μ)/σ; use of tables; normal approximation to binomial (np > 5, nq > 5 with continuity correction); sum of normal variables stays normal.</li></ul>"},
{t:"5. Exam technique for 9709",b:"<ul><li>~11 structured questions escalating in demand; later parts use earlier answers (error carried forward) — NEVER abandon a part.</li><li>Method marks dominate: correct set-up (integral, equation) earns most marks even with arithmetic slips.</li><li>Trig answers: general solution unless a range is given; degrees vs radians — the paper specifies; mixing them is the classic mark killer.</li><li>Stats: define the distribution ('Let X ~ B(20, 0.3)'), state the test, show the calculation, interpret in context ('P(X ≥ 12) = 0.0213 < 0.05, so reject H₀').</li><li>Mechanics: draw the diagram, mark forces, define the positive direction BEFORE equations; answers with units (N, m/s, J).</li><li>Check questions demanding 'exact' answers (leave in π, e, ln, surds) vs 3 s.f.</li></ul>"}],
"alevel/ch9701":[
{t:"1. Physical chemistry — energetics & kinetics",b:"<ul><li>Enthalpy: Hess cycles, bond enthalpies (average, gas phase), calorimetry q = mcΔT with corrections; ionisation energy trends explained by nuclear charge/radius/shielding; electron affinity.</li><li>Kinetics: rate equations from initial-rates tables (orders, k with units), mechanisms (rate-determining step must match the rate equation), Arrhenius: ln k = ln A − Eₐ/RT (gradient of ln k vs 1/T = −Eₐ/R); Maxwell–Boltzmann distribution sketches with Eₐ and T changes; homogeneous vs heterogeneous catalysts.</li><li>Equilibrium: Kc & Kp with ICE tables (mol → concentration via V; partial pressures via mole fractions); units of K; effects of T/P/catalyst on position and on K (only T changes K); industrial contexts (Haber, Contact).</li></ul>"},
{t:"2. Physical chemistry — electrochemistry & acids",b:"<ul><li>Redox: oxidation states & balancing; standard electrode potentials E°; cell emf = E°(cathode) − E°(anode); ΔG = −nFE; feasibility (E° > +0.3 V likely; < −0.3 V not); Nernst idea (concentration effects).</li><li>Electrolysis: products at each electrode (discharge series), quantities Q = It, moles = Q/nF; electroplating/refining calculations.</li><li>Acids & bases: Brønsted theory, Kw, pH of strong/weak acids (weak needs Ka and an ICE table), buffers (Henderson–Hasselbalch pH = pKa + log([salt]/[acid])) and how they resist added acid/base; titration curves (indicators chosen by pH range) and curve shapes for all four acid/base strength combos.</li></ul>"},
{t:"3. Inorganic chemistry",b:"<ul><li>Group 2: reactivity ↑ down (ionisation energy argument), reactions with water/oxygen/acids, solubility trends of hydroxides (↑ down) and sulfates (↓ down) explained by lattice vs hydration enthalpies; flame tests & uses (Mg in alloys, Ca in bones/plaster, BaSO₄ in X-ray 'barium meals').</li><li>Group 17: oxidising power ↓ down (attraction for incoming electron), displacement reactions, reactions with NaOH (cold dilute → chlorate(I); hot concentrated → chlorate(V)) as disproportionation; halide tests (AgNO₃/HNO₃ colours).</li><li>Transition metals: d-block configurations (3d & 4s), definition criteria (ions with partially filled d-subshell), variable oxidation states (Fe²⁺/Fe³⁺, Cu⁺/Cu²⁺, Mn²⁺…MnO₄⁻), COLOURED ions from d-electron transitions (energy gap ΔE = hν; colour absorbed vs observed complementary), catalysts (Fe/Haber, V₂O₅/Contact, Ni/hydrogenation, Pd/Pt hydrogenation), complex ions & ligand exchange (Cu(H₂O)₆²⁺ blue → CuCl₄²⁻ yellow-green; NH₃ stepwise to deep-blue tetraammine), bi-dentate ligands & EDTA; haemoglobin & cisplatin as applied examples.</li></ul>"},
{t:"4. Organic chemistry — the mechanism-first map",b:"<ul><li>Naming & isomerism: IUPAC names, structural/displayed formulae, E/Z with CIP priority rules, optical isomers (chiral centres, plane-polarised light).</li><li>Mechanisms with curly arrows FROM electron pairs: nucleophilic substitution (SN1 tertiary, SN2 primary — know both routes), electrophilic addition (alkene + Br₂/HBr — Markovnikov via carbocation stability), free-radical substitution (alkanes — initiation/propagation/termination), nucleophilic addition to carbonyls (CN⁻ then H⁺ → hydroxynitrile), condensation/esterification, electrophilic substitution of benzene (Br₂/FeBr₃, HNO₃/H₂SO₄ nitration, Friedel–Crafts alkylation/acylation) — delocalised π system attacks the electrophile.</li><li>Synthesis routes: learn the connectivity — alcohol ⇌ alkene → dihalo → dialcohol; oxidation ladders (primary alcohol → aldehyde → acid); nitrile route to amines & carboxylic acids; benzene → phenylamine via nitration/reduction.</li><li>Analysis: IR (O–H broad 3230–3550, C=O ~1700, fingerprint), mass spec (molecular ion = M; fragments), ¹H NMR (chemical shift tables, integration, n+1 splitting, D₂O exchange for –OH), ¹³C NMR (number of environments); combine all three to identify unknowns.</li></ul>"},
{t:"5. Exam technique for 9701",b:"<ul><li>P2 (AS): short structured — definitions word-perfect, calculations show working (Kc answers to 3 s.f. with units), organic mechanisms with correct curly-arrow direction.</li><li>P4 (A2): longer structured — expect multi-step synthesis with reasons ('why is the yield low' → side reactions, volatility, transfer losses), thermodynamics cycles, Born–Haber style reasoning.</li><li>P5: design experiments — identify variables, sketch the graph treatment (e.g., plot log rate vs log concentration → gradient = order), error analysis (systematic vs random, % uncertainty = uncertainty/measurement × 100).</li><li>Definitions bank: activation energy, homolytic vs heterolytic fission, electrophile/nucleophile, standard cell potential, buffer, ligand, dative bond, stereoisomerism.</li><li>Always state assumptions (e.g., 'assuming the acid is monoprotic', 'gas behaves ideally').</li></ul>"}],
"alevel/ph9702":[
{t:"1. Mechanics & matter",b:"<ul><li>Kinematics & dynamics with calculus; projectiles; equilibrium with vector resolution; moments & centre of gravity.</li><li>Momentum, impulse & collisions; work, energy, power (P = Fv); efficiency.</li><li>Matter: density, pressure, upthrust (Archimedes), fluid flow; kinetic theory deriving pV = ⅓Nm⟨c²⟩; r.m.s. speed ∝ √(T/M); Brownian motion evidence.</li><li>Elastic properties: Hooke, Young modulus E = stress/strain, energy stored = ½FΔx; force–extension and stress–strain graphs for brittle/ductile/polymeric materials.</li></ul>"},
{t:"2. Fields — gravitational, electric, magnetic",b:"<ul><li>Gravitation: F = GMm/r², field g = GM/r², potential φ = −GM/r, orbital mechanics v = √(GM/r), T² ∝ r³ (Kepler); geostationary satellites (T = 24 h above equator, r ≈ 42 400 km).</li><li>Electric: F = kQ₁Q₂/r², E = kQ/r² (radial), V = kQ/r; uniform fields E = V/d; capacitors C = εA/d, Q = CV, energy ½CV²; series/parallel; RC discharge V = V₀e^(−t/RC), τ = RC, ln V–t graphs for half-life analogues.</li><li>Magnetic: F = BIL sinθ, F = Bqv; circular motion of charges r = mv/qB; velocity selector; Hall voltage; magnetic flux Φ = BA, Faraday's law ε = −N dΦ/dt, Lenz's law; transformers & the grid (E = I²R argument for high-V transmission).</li></ul>"},
{t:"3. Waves & modern physics",b:"<ul><li>SHM: a = −ω²x, T = 2π√(m/k) = 2π√(L/g); energy interchange; resonance & damping (light/heavy/critical).</li><li>Waves: superposition, interference (path difference nλ vs (n+½)λ), diffraction grating d sinθ = nλ, stationary waves in pipes/strings; Doppler effect f′ = fv/(v ± v_s); ultrasound medical imaging.</li><li>Thermal: first law ΔU = q + w; specific/latent experiments; ideal gas pV = nRT, kinetic theory links; black-body radiation & Wien's law (A2).</li><li>Modern: photoelectric effect E = hf − φ (stopping potential, threshold frequency — particle nature of light); de Broglie λ = h/p; energy levels & line spectra (Bohr); absorption/emission spectra uses in astronomy; radioactive decay N = N₀e^(−λt), t½ = ln2/λ, dating; binding energy curve & E = mc²; fission/fission reactors (moderator, control rods, coolant, critical mass); Hubble & the expanding universe (A2).</li></ul>"},
{t:"4. Medical & applied physics (A2)",b:"<ul><li>X-rays: production (bremsstrahlung & characteristic), attenuation I = I₀e^(−μx), contrast media, CT scanning vs conventional radiography.</li><li>Ultrasound: pulse-echo A/B scans, acoustic impedance Z = ρc, Doppler blood-flow.</li><li>MRI: strong field aligns protons, RF pulses, relaxation signals — superb soft tissue, no ionising radiation.</li><li>PET: positron annihilation γ detection; tracers & gamma cameras.</li><li>Lasers & fibre optics in medicine/endoscopy.</li></ul>"},
{t:"5. Exam technique for 9702",b:"<ul><li>Definitions word-perfect ('the rate of change of momentum', 'work done per unit charge'); explain = mechanism chains.</li><li>Diagrams: field lines, circuits, ray diagrams labelled; graphs with labelled axes and quantified intercepts.</li><li>Calculations: formula → rearrange → substitute with units → answer 2–3 s.f.; powers-of-ten discipline with prefixes.</li><li>P5 experiment design: variables stated, procedure enabling repeats, graph manipulation to linearise (e.g., plot T² vs m → gradient 4π²/k).</li><li>Estimate questions: order-of-magnitude reasoning earns marks — always attempt.</li></ul>"}],
"alevel/bio9700":[
{t:"1. Cell biology & membranes",b:"<ul><li>Ultrastructure from EM images; magnification calculations; cytoskeleton; endosymbiotic theory evidence (mitochondria/chloroplasts: double membranes, own DNA/ribosomes).</li><li>Membrane structure (fluid mosaic): phospholipid bilayer, proteins (carrier/channel/receptor/glycoprotein roles), cholesterol; factors on diffusion/osmosis/active transport rates; facilitated diffusion vs active transport (ATP, against gradient).</li><li>Cell division: mitosis stages & significance (growth/repair/asexual), meiosis & variation; stem cells (totipotent → pluripotent), therapeutic cloning debate.</li><li>Enzymes deep: Vmax & Km from Michaelis–Menten, competitive vs non-competitive inhibition (Km shifts), immobilised enzymes (industry: pectinase in juice, lactase in milk).</li></ul>"},
{t:"2. Biochemistry & transport",b:"<ul><li>Water & hydrogen bonding in biology; carbohydrates (mono/di/polysaccharides: α-glucose vs β-glucose — cellulose straight chains vs starch amylose helix); proteins (primary→quaternary, R-groups, denaturation); lipids (triglycerides, phospholipids, test emulsions).</li><li>DNA: structure (Watson–Crick), base pairing, replication semi-conservative (Meselson–Stahl), protein synthesis outline: transcription (nucleus) → mRNA → translation at ribosomes (codons/anticodons); mutations & consequences (sickle cell as case).</li><li>Gas transport: haemoglobin loading/unloading curves, Bohr shift, fetal haemoglobin; carbonic anhydrase & the chloride shift.</li><li>Heart & ECG; xylem transport (cohesion–tension, root pressure, capillarity), transpiration factors (humidity/wind/light/temperature experiments with potometer); phloem mass flow (source → sink, bidirectional evidence from aphid stylets & ringing).</li><li>Immunity: phagocytosis, clonal selection of lymphocytes, antibodies (structure & agglutination), vaccination & herd immunity; HIV lifecycle & immunosuppression.</li></ul>"},
{t:"3. Energy (A2 core)",b:"<ul><li><b>Respiration:</b> glycolysis (cytoplasm, net 2 ATP, 2 NADH) → link reaction → Krebs cycle (CO₂, NADH, FADH₂, 1 ATP) → oxidative phosphorylation (ETC + chemiosmosis in cristae; ~28–30 ATP total); anaerobic in mammals vs yeast; respiratory substrates compared (carbohydrate vs lipid RQ).</li><li><b>Photosynthesis:</b> light-dependent stage (thylakoids: photoactivation of PSII, photolysis of water, electron transport → chemiosmosis → ATP & reduced NADP) and Calvin cycle (stroma: CO₂ fixation by RuBP/rubisco → GP → TP using ATP & reduced NADP; TP → glucose or regenerate RuBP); limiting factors experiments (algal balls, pondweed, oxygen electrode); C4 & CAM as adaptations (A2 context).</li><li>Both processes compared: membranes as energy-transducing systems; ATP the universal currency.</li></ul>"},
{t:"4. Control, inheritance & evolution (A2)",b:"<ul><li>Nervous control: resting/action potentials (sodium–potassium pump, voltage-gated channels), saltatory conduction, synapse transmission (Ca²⁺, neurotransmitter, receptors); drug action at synapses (nicotine, MDMA).</li><li>Homeostasis: thermoregulation, blood glucose (insulin/glucagon second-messenger mechanism), kidney & ADH/osmoregulation; liver as homeostatic hub.</li><li>Plant hormones: auxins & phototropism/gravitropism (differential elongation), gibberellins & stem elongation/dormancy, ethene ripening, abscisic acid stomatal control; commercial applications.</li><li>Genetics: test crosses, codominance & multiple alleles (ABO), sex linkage (colour blindness, haemophilia), autosomal linkage & cross-over values, epistasis ratios (9:3:3:1 variants), chi-squared tests; Hardy–Weinberg p² + 2pq + q² = 1 & selection calculations; speciation (allopatric vs sympatric), selection types (stabilising/directional/disruptive); artificial selection & GMOs (vector plasmids, restriction enzymes, ligase, marker genes); PCR & gel electrophoresis; gene therapy & screening ethics.</li><li>Ecosystems: energy flow & ecological pyramids, nutrient cycles (carbon, nitrogen), succession, conservation management, populations & limiting factors (sigmoid growth, r vs K strategists).</li></ul>"},
{t:"5. Exam technique for 9700",b:"<ul><li>Data questions dominate: quote numbers from the graph/table in your explanation ('as CO₂ rises from 0.03% to 0.1%, rate rises from 10 to 22 bubbles/min, because…').</li><li>'Explain' chains: mechanism with because/therefore; link structure → function every time.</li><li>P3 practical: familiarise with microscope measuring (eyepiece graticule calibration), drawings (plan & detail rules), enzyme/kinetics/photosynthesis standard experiments.</li><li>P5: chi-squared (state H₀, expected from ratio, Σ(O−E)²/E, compare to critical value at p = 0.05, conclude); design questions — control variables ×3, repeats & mean, named statistical test with justification.</li><li>Definitions: diffusion/osmosis/active transport (word-perfect), photosynthesis, respiration, homeostasis, negative feedback, transpiration, gene/allele/locus.</li></ul>"}],
"alevel/acc9706":[
{t:"1. Financial accounting core",b:"<ul><li>Double-entry mastery assumed from O Level; focus on errors, suspense, and control accounts with complex adjustments (contra, refunds, dishonoured cheques, interest on overdue accounts).</li><li>Partnerships: capital vs current accounts, appropriation statement, goodwill on admission/retirement/death (revaluation account method), loans from partners (interest = expense).</li><li>Companies: bonus & rights issues (share premium movements), debentures vs shares (interest vs dividend; tax treatment), dividends (interim/final/proposed), reserves & their uses, bonus issue from share premium ONLY.</li><li>Incomplete records: control-account & 'total' methods, cash summaries, markup/margin conversions, statement of affairs.</li><li>Manufacturing accounts: prime cost → factory overheads (under/over-absorption) → cost of production; income statement presentation for a manufacturer.</li></ul>"},
{t:"2. Analysis & interpretation",b:"<ul><li>Ratio suite with formulas & verdicts: profitability (gross margin, operating margin, ROCE, ROE), liquidity (current, acid test, cash flow), efficiency (asset turnover, inventory days, receivables days, payables days — the working-capital cycle), gearing (debt/equity, interest cover), investment (EPS, dividend yield, P/E).</li><li>Trend + comparative analysis ('gross margin fell from 32% to 27% — possibly discounting to clear inventory, supported by inventory days rising 45→68').</li><li>Limitations: inflation, one-off items, creative accounting, sector differences, seasonal trading.</li><li>Cash flow statements (IAS 7 indirect method): operating (profit + depreciation − gains ± working capital), investing, financing; reconciling profit to cash movement — the classic 'why profitable but no cash' answer.</li><li>Consolidated basics (if in syllabus year): goodwill, non-controlling interest, intra-group trading & unrealised profit.</li></ul>"},
{t:"3. Cost & management accounting",b:"<ul><li>Cost classification & behaviour (fixed/stepped/variable/semi-variable with high-low method); overhead absorption: allocation → apportionment → reapportionment (reciprocal services) → absorption by OAR; under/over absorption explained & adjusted.</li><li>Marginal costing: contribution per unit/unit limiting factor; make-or-buy; special orders (relevant costing — ignore sunk costs & unavoidable fixed costs); break-even & margin of safety multi-product (C/S ratio).</li><li>Absorption vs marginal profit reconciliation (inventory change × fixed overhead per unit).</li><li>Process costing: normal loss (scrap value credit), abnormal loss/gain accounts, equivalent units for WIP; joint products vs by-products (split-off, NRV method).</li><li>Job/batch/service costing features.</li><li>Standard costing & variance analysis: material price/usage, labour rate/efficiency, variable overhead, fixed overhead expenditure/volume, sales price/volume; interdependence (favourable price + adverse usage = cheaper poorer material); basic variance investigation rules.</li><li>Budgeting: functional budgets → cash budget → master budget; flexed budgets for control; behavioural aspects (budgetary slack, participation).</li><li>Investment appraisal: NPV (discount tables, accept > 0), IRR (interpolation), payback & discounted payback, ARR; qualitative factors; sensitivity (which variable kills NPV first).</li></ul>"},
{t:"4. Exam technique for 9706",b:"<ul><li>P2 (90 marks, answer structured questions): formats earn presentation marks — ruled, headed, dated; workings columns for every calculated figure.</li><li>Own-figure rule everywhere: method marks survive arithmetic slips; never leave parts blank.</li><li>P3 (A2 structured): cash flow statement order memorised (operating → investing → financing); ratio questions demand COMPARISON + consequence.</li><li>P4 (Cost & Management): show formulas (contribution per unit, OAR, NPV layout); state assumptions; discount factors copied correctly.</li><li>MCQ: watch double negatives, 'which is NOT', and units (per unit vs total).</li></ul>"}],
"alevel/bus9609":[
{t:"1. Enterprise & human resources",b:"<ul><li>Entrepreneurship & intrapreneurship; business planning (the living document); economic sectors & their shifts; stakeholders mapping & conflicting objectives (shareholders vs community vs environment).</li><li>Organisational design: tall/flat/matrix/entrepreneurial (Burns & Stalker contingency); delegation, span of control, centralisation; organisational culture (Handy's types) & cultural web.</li><li>HR planning: labour turnover formula & remedies; recruitment economics (costs of bad hire); selection validity (interviews' weaknesses — halo effect, structured alternatives: assessment centres, psychometrics, work sampling).</li><li>Motivation in depth: content theories (Maslow, Herzberg, McClelland's need for achievement) vs process theories (Vroom's expectancy: effort → performance → reward valence; Adams' equity; Locke's goal-setting SMART); financial motivators (piece rate, commission, profit-sharing, ESOPs, fringe benefits) vs non-financial (job rotation/enlargement/enrichment, empowerment, quality circles, flexible working, teamworking).</li><li>Leadership: style continua (Tannenbaum–Schmidt), contingency models (Fiedler: task vs relationship orientation matched to situation; Hersey–Blanchard readiness), transformational vs transactional (Bass), charismatic & its risks.</li><li>Communication: channels & richness, barriers, networks; conflict & change management (Kotter's 8 steps, Lewin's force-field).</li></ul>"},
{t:"2. Marketing & operations",b:"<ul><li>Marketing strategy: segmentation (bases & effective segmentation tests), targeting (undifferentiated/concentrated/differentiated), positioning maps; marketing planning & budgets; digital marketing metrics (click-through, conversion, cost-per-acquisition); relationship marketing & loyalty schemes.</li><li>Product decisions: Boston matrix (question marks→stars→cash cows→dogs strategies), product life cycle extension strategies, branding & packaging psychology, new product development stages.</li><li>Pricing: elasticity-informed decisions, price discrimination conditions, loss leaders, psychological pricing, transfer pricing basics; competitor-based pricing in oligopoly (kinked demand idea).</li><li>Distribution: channel length decisions, vertical/horizontal integration, franchising, e-commerce disintermediation.</li><li>Operations: capacity utilisation & its economics; lean production (5S, kaizen, JIT, TQM — Deming, quality circles, benchmarking, Six Sigma overview); network analysis (CPA: earliest/latest start, float, crashing); R&D, patents; technology (CAD/CAM/CIM/AI/robotics) and its HR implications.</li><li>Inventory management beyond EOQ: JIT partnerships, safety stock maths; quality costs (prevention vs appraisal vs internal/external failure).</li></ul>"},
{t:"3. Finance & strategic analysis (A2)",b:"<ul><li>Financial: cash-flow forecasting & remedies; working capital cycle & optimisation; budgets & variance; final accounts & ratio suite (as 9706 but applied to business decisions: 'should the bank extend the overdraft?'); sources of finance matched to need & gearing effects.</li><li>Strategic tools: mission/vision/objectives (SMART); SWOT & TOWS; PESTLE; Porter's Five Forces (industry attractiveness); Porter's generic strategies (cost leadership, differentiation, focus) & stuck-in-the-middle; Ansoff; Boston; core competence (Prahalad & Hamel); benchmarking; the value chain (primary vs support activities); synergy & the 7-S framework as integration checklists.</li><li>Growth: organic vs external (mergers: horizontal/vertical/conglomerate; takeovers; MBOs; joint ventures; strategic alliances); integration motives & failure rates (culture clash, overpayment, due diligence).</li><li>Globalisation vs localisation (glocalisation); market entry modes (export → licensing → franchising → JV → FDI); trading blocs & WTO; exchange-rate exposure (transaction/translation/economic) & hedging basics.</li><li>Ethics, CSR & ESG: Friedman vs Freeman (shareholder vs stakeholder); triple bottom line; Friedman: 'the social responsibility of business is to increase profits' (within rules) vs Porter & Kramer's 'shared value'; whistleblowing, governance codes.</li><li>Contingency planning, crisis management, business continuity.</li></ul>"},
{t:"4. Exam technique for 9609",b:"<ul><li>Levels of response: K → APPL (case facts) → AN (because/therefore chains) → EVAL (two sides + committed verdict). A 12-marker without conclusion caps ~6.</li><li>P1 (1 h 15, 40): Section A short questions test precise knowledge (formulas, theory names, examples) — answer crisply; Section B one question with 8 & 12 mark parts.</li><li>P2 (1 h 30, 60): data response — quote the case data (exhibits!) in every developed paragraph; calculation parts show working.</li><li>P3 (3 h, 100): the big one. Essay plans: define → apply framework → case evidence → counter-argument → verdict with numbers ('with $2m cash per Exhibit 3, diversification is affordable but…').</li><li>Memorise a template paragraph per strategy tool so the exam is assembly, not invention.</li><li>Timing discipline: 1.8 min/mark on P3 — plan 5 min/essay.</li></ul>"}],
"alevel/eco9708":[
{t:"1. Micro — markets & elasticity",b:"<ul><li>Scarcity, choice, opportunity cost; PPF (points inside = unemployment, on = efficient, outside = unattainable; growth shifts it out).</li><li>Demand & supply: shifts vs movements; determinants; consumer & producer surplus; price elasticity PED = %ΔQd/%ΔP (determinants: substitutes, necessity, income share, time) with revenue-rule applications; PES; YED (normal/inferior); XED (substitutes/complements).</li><li>Intervention: maximum prices (shortages → queues/rationing/black markets) & minimum prices (surpluses → buffer stocks); indirect taxes (statutory vs economic incidence — falls heavier on inelastic side) & subsidies; PED determines effectiveness of 'sin taxes'.</li></ul>"},
{t:"2. Micro — market failure & the firm",b:"<ul><li>Market failure types: externalities (MSC vs MSB diagrams, welfare triangles), public goods (non-rival, non-excludable — free-rider), merit/demerit goods (information gaps), factor immobility (geographical/occupational), inequality, monopoly power.</li><li>Government responses: taxes/subsidies, regulation, permits (tradable pollution permits), state provision, information campaigns, nudges; government failure (imperfect information, unintended consequences, administrative costs, political cycles).</li><li>Costs & revenue: TC = TFC + TVC; AC, MC, AFC geometry; normal vs supernormal profit; profit max MR = MC.</li><li>Perfect competition: price-taker, MR = P, long-run normal profit, allocative & productive efficiency — the benchmark.</li><li>Monopoly: downward AR & MR, price > MC, deadweight loss, X-inefficiency, rent-seeking; natural monopoly; price discrimination (conditions, consumer surplus capture); regulation (price capping RPI−X, profit regulation, nationalisation vs privatisation).</li><li>Monopolistic competition & oligopoly: kinked demand (price rigidity), game theory (prisoner's dilemma, Nash, collusion vs competition — cartels & their instability), contestable markets (Baumol — threat of entry disciplines).</li><li>Monopsony (single buyer: NHS as labour monopsonist — minimum wage can raise BOTH wages and employment, the diagram worth memorising).</li><li>Labour market: MRP theory, demand for labour = MRP; supply determinants; wage differentials (MRP vs non-pecuniary factors); transfer earnings vs economic rent.</li></ul>"},
{t:"3. Macro — the big models",b:"<ul><li>National income: GDP/GNP/NNP, real vs nominal (GDP deflator), per capita & its limits (HDI alternative); circular flow (injections J = I + G + X, withdrawals W = S + T + M; equilibrium Y when J = W).</li><li>AD/AS: AD components C+I+G+(X−M); SRAS shapes (Keynesian horizontal→vertical, classical vertical); shifts & macro outcomes (growth, demand-pull vs cost-push inflation, deflationary gaps).</li><li>Inflation: CPI measurement, causes (demand-pull, cost-push, monetary, imported), consequences (menu/shoe-leather, redistribution, uncertainty, competitiveness) & anti-inflation policy trade-offs (Phillips short-run; expectations-augmented long-run vertical at NRU — Friedman & Phelps).</li><li>Unemployment: types (frictional, structural, cyclical/demand-deficient, seasonal, technological, voluntary) & costs (lost output Okun, fiscal cost, social); policies targeted per type.</li><li>Balance of payments: current account components (trade in goods/services, primary & secondary income); causes of deficits (competitiveness, growth differentials, structural); corrections (expenditure-reducing: deflation; expenditure-switching: devaluation — Marshall–Lerner PEDx + PEDm > 1 & the J-curve; supply-side).</li><li>Exchange rates: floating determination, fixed/managed systems; appreciation vs depreciation effects (BOP, inflation, growth); currency unions criteria (optimum currency area).</li></ul>"},
{t:"4. Policy & the global economy (A2)",b:"<ul><li>Fiscal policy: budget stances, automatic stabilisers vs discretionary; multiplier k = 1/(MPS+MPT+MPM) & its limitations (crowding out, supply constraints, expectations/ricardian equivalence); public debt sustainability; Laffer curve (tax rate vs revenue).</li><li>Monetary policy: interest-rate transmission (policy rate → borrowing/saving → AD → inflation; asset prices & expectations channels); quantitative easing; rules vs discretion; time lags; liquidity trap (Keynes) & the zero lower bound.</li><li>Supply-side policies: market-based (deregulation, privatisation, tax cuts, reducing union power, education/training) vs interventionist (industrial policy, infrastructure, R&D grants); evaluation: time lags, cost, conflicts (inflation vs growth vs BOP), effectiveness depends on the cause of the problem.</li><li>Policy conflicts & the policy mix; the 'impossible trinity' (fixed FX + free capital + independent monetary policy — pick two).</li><li>International & development: comparative advantage & the gains from trade (opportunity cost ratios), terms of trade; free trade vs protection (tariff diagrams, quota, subsidies, VERs; infant industry, strategic, dumping, health/environment arguments; WTO rounds, trading blocs — trade creation vs diversion; globalisation drivers & critics).</li><li>Development economics: growth vs development (GDP vs HDI vs multidimensional poverty); population & development (demographic transition, dependency ratios, the demographic dividend); barriers (debt, primary product dependence — Prebisch–Singer declining terms of trade, poor infrastructure, institutions); strategies (import substitution vs export orientation, FDI, aid — types & effectiveness debate, microfinance, fair trade, tourism); role of IMF/World Bank/WTO; sustainable development goals.</li></ul>"},
{t:"5. Exam technique for 9708",b:"<ul><li>Essay engine (8/12/20 marks): define key terms → analysis with a fully labelled diagram (axes, curves, equilibria, shifts, shaded areas) → chain of reasoning (because/therefore) → evaluation (depends on: elasticity, time period, magnitude, economy's state) → committed conclusion.</li><li>Data response: USE the extract data — quote numbers ('exports fell 14% in Q2'); never merely restate.</li><li>12-mark structure suggestion: intro + 3 analysis paragraphs + 2 evaluation points + conclusion ≈ 2 pages.</li><li>P4's 20-marker is 1/3 of the paper — practise two full 20-mark essays weekly under 30 minutes each.</li><li>MCQs: definitions & calculation drills (elasticity, multiplier, % changes) — speed wins P1 & P3.</li></ul>"}],
"alevel/psy9990":[
{t:"1. Approaches & core studies",b:"<ul><li>The 12 core studies (know aim, method, results, conclusion, TWO criticisms each): Milgram (obedience 65% to 450V — agentic state, authority legitimacy, proximity variations) · Bandura (Bobo doll — social learning, 88% imitation) · Andrade (doodling aids concentration) · Baron-Cohen (eyes test, adults with AS/HFA — theory of mind) · Dement & Kleitman (REM & dream recall — objective measurement) · Hölzel (8-week mindfulness → grey-matter changes — neuroplasticity) · Piliavin (subway helping — diffusion of responsibility, model effect) · Saavedra & Silverman (button phobia — clinical single-case method) · Fagen (elephant operant conditioning) · Pozzulo (child line-up accuracy — own-age bias?) · Hassett (vervet monkey toy preferences — biology vs socialisation) · Perry (intranasal oxytocin & trust games).</li><li>Approaches: learning (behaviourism, social learning — Bandura's modelling with mediating cognitive factors), cognitive (computer analogy, schema), biological (genetics, neurochemistry, evolution), psychodynamic (Freud — repression, the unconscious; evaluation), humanistic (Maslow, Rogers — free will, self-actualisation).</li><li>Issues & debates: nature/nurture, reductionism vs holism, determinism vs free will, individual vs situational explanations, ethics (Milgram's legacy → modern codes), usefulness, validity (internal/external/ecological/population), reliability (test–retest, inter-rater), generalisability, the quant vs qual tension, social sensitivity.</li></ul>"},
{t:"2. Research methods in depth",b:"<ul><li>Experimental designs: independent groups (no order effects; participant variables), repeated measures (fewer participants; order effects → counterbalancing), matched pairs (controls both; costly matching). Lab/field/natural/quasi — control vs ecological validity trade-off.</li><li>Observations: covert vs overt, participant vs non-participant, structured (behavioural categories, event/time sampling) vs unstructured; inter-rater reliability checks.</li><li>Self-reports: questionnaires (open/closed, Likert scales, postal/online — sampling bias toward volunteers) & interviews (structured/semi/unstructured; social desirability, interviewer bias, leading questions).</li><li>Case studies: rich idiographic data, longitudinal, rare conditions; generalisability & retrospective bias limits.</li><li>Correlations: co-variables, direction/strength from scattergrams (±0.7 strong etc.), NEVER causation; multiple correlations confounding.</li><li>Sampling: random, systematic, stratified, opportunity, volunteer — match method to aim; sample size vs representativeness debates.</li><li>Hypotheses: null vs experimental/alternative, directional vs non-directional (two-tailed default unless strong prior theory); variables: IV/DV operationalised; extraneous vs confounding.</li><li>Analysis: descriptive (mean/median/mode; range/SD — SD as average deviation) & inferential — choosing the test: chi-squared (nominal, frequency), Spearman (correlation, ordinal), Mann-Whitney (unrelated difference, ordinal+), Wilcoxon (related difference, ordinal+); significance at p ≤ 0.05 (Type I/II errors: 0.01 cuts Type I, raises Type II); observed vs critical values & the rejection rule (remember Mann-Whitney/Wilcoxon/Spearman direction rules!).</li><li>Ethics: BPS code — consent, deception, right to withdraw, debrief, confidentiality, protection from harm; cost–benefit reasoning & how famous studies violated each (Milgram: deception & harm; Zimbardo: withdrawal rights).</li></ul>"},
{t:"3. Specialist options — abnormal & health",b:"<ul><li><b>Abnormal psychology:</b> definitions of abnormality (statistical infrequency, deviation from social norms, failure to function, DSM/ICD classification — reliability & validity debates, cultural bias Rosenhan); schizophrenia & depression: biological explanations (genetics twin/concordance, dopamine hypothesis, neuroanatomy) vs psychological (family dysfunction, cognitive distortions Beck, learned helplessness Seligman); treatments: antipsychotics & antidepressants (SSRI mechanism, evaluation) vs CBT & family therapy; effectiveness & side-effect comparisons; the medical model critique (Szasz — 'myth of mental illness').</li><li><b>Health psychology:</b> adherence to medical advice (factors: complexity, side effects, beliefs — Health Belief Model: perceived susceptibility, seriousness, benefits, barriers + self-efficacy; improving adherence: simplifying regimes, reminders, patient education, practitioner communication); pain (gate control theory — psychological modulation; measuring pain; chronic pain management CBT & biofeedback); stress & illness (Selye's GAS: alarm–resistance–exhaustion; immune suppression link; Type A & heart disease Friedman & Rosenman); practitioner–patient communication (doctor-centred vs patient-centred; compliance outcomes).</li></ul>"},
{t:"4. Specialist options — sport, work & more",b:"<ul><li><b>Sport & exercise:</b> motivation (intrinsic vs extrinsic, achievement goal theory, self-efficacy Bandura's four sources), arousal & performance (inverted-U; catastrophe model nods), anxiety (state vs trait; pre-competition routines), teamwork & cohesion (Steiner's model — actual productivity = potential − faulty processes), leadership styles in coaching, home advantage explanations.</li><li><b>Work & organisational:</b> groups at work (formation stages Tuckman: forming–storming–norming–performing; groupthink Janis; social loafing Ringelmann), leadership (Fiedler contingency, transactional vs transformational applied to workplace), job satisfaction & measurement, organisational culture & change.</li><li><b>Child psychology (option):</b> prenatal development, attachment (Bowlby, Ainsworth's strange situation, deprivation vs privation — Romanian adoptees), deprivation's long-term effects, day care debates.</li><li><b>Cognitive (option):</b> memory models (multi-store, working memory), reconstructive memory (Loftus & Palmer's 'smashed' study — leading questions), eyewitness testimony reform.</li><li><b>Law (option):</b> eyewitness factors (estimator vs system variables Wells), lineup procedures (sequential vs simultaneous), jury decision-making, offender profiling.</li><li><b>Environment & consumer (options):</b> noise/crowding effects, conservation behaviour, advertising persuasion.</li></ul>"},
{t:"5. Exam technique for 9990",b:"<ul><li>P1: Section A — study-specific questions (result? conclusion? one criticism?); Section B — essay ('Discuss the contribution of the learning approach…') → describe two studies + evaluate strengths/limits + compare with another approach.</li><li>P2: scenario methods questions — identify the design, name the sampling, spot the confounding variable, justify a statistical test (name + why: level of measurement, related/unrelated, difference/correlation), redesign improvements; PLUS a 10-mark 'plan an investigation' with aim, hypotheses, design, sample, procedure, controls, ethics, analysis plan.</li><li>P3/P4 options: theory questions need named theories + studies + evaluation; application questions mirror the scenario; plan questions mirror P2's design format.</li><li>Evaluation toolkit phrases: 'low ecological validity because…', 'demand characteristics as participants may have guessed…', 'social desirability may have inflated…', 'the sample of 20 American undergraduates limits generalisability to…', 'reliability is supported by inter-rater agreement of 0.9'.</li><li>Memorise the decision tree for tests: nominal? → chi-squared · correlation? → Spearman · related difference? → Wilcoxon · unrelated difference? → Mann-Whitney.</li></ul>"}],
"alevel/eng9093":[
{t:"1. Textual analysis toolkit",b:"<ul><li>Levels framework: LEXIS (word choice, semantic fields, connotation, register, archaisms, neologisms, dialect/accent marking) · GRAMMAR (syntax length & complexity, sentence moods declarative/interrogative/imperative/exclamative, modality — modal verbs & hedging, pronoun choices I/we/you = stance & solidarity, active vs passive, transitivity) · FIGURES (metaphor, simile, personification, hyperbole, irony, allusion) · PHONOLOGY (alliteration, assonance, sibilance, rhythm, rhyme, onomatopoeia — in speech also pitch, stress, pace, pause, volume) · DISCOURSE (structure: headline→orientation→complication; cohesion devices: anaphora, conjunctions, lexical chains; Grice's maxims & implicature; speech acts; politeness theory Brown & Levinson; turn-taking, adjacency pairs, overlaps, interruptions, hedging in speech).</li><li>Commentary method: select 3–4 significant features per text, quote precisely, analyse effect, link to AUDIENCE & PURPOSE & CONTEXT; COMPARE across the pair (structure by feature or by text — keep the comparison live: 'whereas Text A…, Text B…').</li><li>Genre & mode: spoken vs written continua (spontaneity markers: fillers, false starts, contractions, ellipsis, non-fluency); multimodality (images, layout, font — adverts, posters).</li></ul>"},
{t:"2. Writing & directed tasks",b:"<ul><li>Directed writing: transform stimulus text(s) into a new genre/register for a new audience — the brief's audience/purpose/format marks are checklist items; plan the stance (support/oppose/nuance) and sustain it.</li><li>Composition: discursive/argumentative (thesis → arguments → counter-argument → verdict) & descriptive/narrative (same craft as O Level upgraded: controlled symbolism, narrative voice, structural arcs).</li><li>Style commentary upgrade: comment on your OWN rhetorical choices (why the tricolon? why the switch to second person?) — this meta-awareness is the A-level separator.</li></ul>"},
{t:"3. Language topics — global English & acquisition",b:"<ul><li><b>World Englishes:</b> Kachru's three circles (inner: UK/US/Aus/NZ/Canada · outer: India, Nigeria, Singapore, Pakistan — institutionalised second-language Englishes · expanding: China, Japan, Egypt — EFL). Features of outer-circle varieties (Indian English: 'prepone', reduplication 'small-small'; Nigerian English; Singapore's 'lah'; African American Vernacular features); codification debates (dictionaries of world Englishes), standard language ideology & prestige; English as a Lingua Franca (ELF) — Jenkins' core (pronunciation features needed for intelligibility: consonant sounds, vowel length, nuclear stress) — challenges native-speaker norms; ownership of English (Widdowson: 'the language has become the common property of its users').</li><li><b>Child language acquisition:</b> pre-linguistic stages (crying → cooing → babbling 6 months → proto-words); phonological development (substitution, cluster reduction, fronting, deletion — learn the technical terms with examples); lexical development (overextension 'doggie' for all animals; underextension); grammatical development (holophrases → two-word stage ~18 months → telegraphic speech → morpheme acquisition order Brown's 14 morphemes: '-ing', plural, past irregular, possessive, articles…; overgeneralisation 'goed', 'mouses', 'sheeps'); pragmatic development (turn-taking routines, politeness); theories: behaviourist (Skinner — imitation & reinforcement; limits: novelty & overgeneralisation can't be imitated), innatist (Chomsky — LAD & universal grammar; poverty of the stimulus; the logical problem), interactionist (Bruner — LASS: scaffolding, CDS/child-directed speech features: higher pitch, repetition, simplified grammar, expansion; Vygotsky's ZPD), cognitive (Piaget — language needs cognitive readiness).</li><li>Evidence evaluation: CDS correlational not causal; overgeneralisation evidence for rule-learning; twin studies & 'wolf children' (Genie — critical period hypothesis Lenneberg: lateralisation by puberty).</li></ul>"},
{t:"4. Exam technique for 9093",b:"<ul><li>P1 (Reading): comprehension with own-words discipline + a summary + language analysis — same craft as 0500 deepened; timing ~2 h 15 for 50 marks.</li><li>P2 (Writing): directed + composition; register precision and rhetorical control are the band differentiators.</li><li>P3 (Language Analysis): unseen pair — 15 minutes reading & annotating before writing; organise by feature clusters; every paragraph ends with effect-on-reader-in-context.</li><li>P4 (Topics): knowledge questions (theories with names & dates/studies) + applied analysis of data (transcript conventions: (.), (1), CAPS for emphasis; interpret don't transcribe). Learn 4–5 transcript excerpts as practice.</li><li>Quote briefly & precisely; terminology deployed accurately scores higher than feature-spotting volume.</li></ul>"}],
"alevel/pak9389":[
{t:"1. History of Pakistan since 1947 — politics in depth",b:"<ul><li>Constitutional chronology: Objectives Resolution 1949 → 1956 (first; parliamentary; abrogated 1958) → 1962 (Ayub presidential, indirect Basic Democracies) → 1973 (Bhutto; parliamentary; Islamic provisions: Council of Islamic Ideology, Islam as state religion, ushr/zakat later; still current) → 8th Amendment 1985 (58(2)(b) presidential dissolution power) → 13th 1997 (stripped it) → 17th 2003 (restored) → 18th 2010 (parliamentary supremacy, provincial autonomy via devolved subjects & 7th NFC Award).</li><li>Civil–military relations: four martial laws (1958, 1969, 1977, 1999) — causes & consequences compared; the 'security state' thesis vs democratic consolidation debate.</li><li>Leadership studies: Jinnah (constitutionalist vision), Liaquat (Objectives Resolution, assassination 1951), Ayub (growth vs inequality), Yahya (1971 responsibility debate: political failure vs military blunder), Bhutto (populism, 1973 constitution, nationalisation legacy, controversial end), Zia (Islamisation's institutional legacy), Benazir & Nawaz (corruption allegations vs development records), Musharraf (9/11 pivot, Kargil legacy).</li><li>1971 in depth: economic disparity (East's export earnings vs West's spending), political exclusion (One Unit neutering Bengal's majority), language imposition, military operation's human cost, Indian role, Hamoodur Rahman Commission, lasting institutional lessons.</li><li>Federalism & provinces: One Unit 1955–70 & its scars; provincial autonomy movements (Bengali 1948–71, Sindhi 1970s–80s, Baloch grievances — insurgencies 1973–77 & post-2005, ANP/PTM Pashtun politics); GB & AJK constitutional limbo.</li></ul>"},
{t:"2. Economy of Pakistan in depth",b:"<ul><li>Phases: colonial extraction → early instability (food aid dependence) → Ayub's green revolution & import-substitution industrialisation → Bhutto's nationalisation (1972–76: 32 units — inefficiency & investment flight debate) → Zia's denationalisation & Afghan-war aid boom → 1988–99 stop-go cycles, IMF programmes I & II → Musharraf growth (consumption-led, 2002–07) → 2008 crises (balance of payments, power) → CPEC era (2015–) & recurring stabilization (2019, 2023 IMF).</li><li>Structural issues: narrow tax base (tax-to-GDP ~9–10%, one of the world's lowest; the elite exemption culture — land, retail, agriculture), energy circular debt, loss-making SOEs (PIA, Pakistan Steel), export concentration (textiles), import compression cycles, informal (cash) economy ~30–40%, brain drain.</li><li>Agriculture deep-dive: land tenure (zamindari legacies; ceilings & reforms attempts), water productivity (lowest per unit among peers), crop vs livestock economics, food security (wheat procurement paradox: farmer price vs consumer subsidy), climate vulnerability.</li><li>Industry & services: textile value-chain gaps (spinning-heavy; knitwear/home-textile upgrade), Sialkot clusters, IT freelancing boom, banking & telecom growth, capital markets thinness.</li><li>CPEC economics: $60bn+ commitments; power projects debt vs generation gain; SEZs progress (Rashakai, Dhabeji, Allama Iqbal), Gwadar's port + airport + free zone — opportunity (western China access, regional hub) vs debt-trap critique & transparency demands; lessons from Sri Lanka discourse.</li><li>Remittances, diaspora & the Gulf corridor; FDI vs portfolio flows; rupee management history (managed float 1982 onwards; multiple IMF-linked depreciations).</li></ul>"},
{t:"3. Society, culture & foreign policy in depth",b:"<ul><li><b>Society:</b> population dynamics (growth from 32m in 1947 to 240m; the missed demographic-policy windows; the 2023 census politics); urban transformation (Karachi's megacity governance failures vs Lahore's development; smart city rhetoric); education emergency (out-of-school children ~26m; madrassa vs mainstream divide; NSP/ Single National Curriculum controversy); health (polio eradication battle — attacks on vaccinators as case study of extremism's social cost; hepatitis & diabetes burdens; health cards experiment).</li><li><b>Culture & identity:</b> the 'Ideology of Pakistan' debate (Two-Nation Theory post-1971 re-readings); Islamisation's layering (Objectives Resolution → Zia's ordinances → curriculum effects); regional literatures (Sindhi renaissance Shah Latif, Punjabi Bulleh Shah, Pashto Rahman Baba — Sufi humanism as counter-extremism resource); media evolution (PTV monopoly → 89 channels → social media's disruption & disinformation crises); sports politics (India cricket boycotts, hybrid tournaments).</li><li><b>Foreign policy doctrine:</b> founding principles (Muslim solidarity, anti-imperialism, UN charter membership, Kashmir) → alliance era (SEATO/CENTO 1954–55: aid & the 1965 embargo lesson) → post-1965 'bilateralism' & 1972 Simla framework → 1979 Afghan pivot (Zia's strategic depth; the madrassa-militant externality) → 1998 nuclearisation (deterrence doctrine, NPT refusal logic) → post-9/11 'frontline state' paradox (aid vs sovereignty costs; drone debate) → ' geo-economic' pivot under Imran Khan → reset diplomacy 2022– (multi-alignment: US engagement + China deep + Russia thaw + Gulf financial ties + Saudi-Iran balancing).</li><li>Indo-Pak relations anatomy: Kashmir dispute frameworks (UN resolutions vs Simla bilateralism vs Musharraf four-point formula), CBMs (Delhi-Lahore bus 1999, cross-LOC trade 2008, Kartarpur 2019), crisis escalations (Kargil 1999, Parliament attack 2001 standoff, Pulwama-Balakot 2019 — the nuclear-shadow escalation management debate), the dialogue-freeze era & its costs.</li><li>Afghan policy: Durand Line non-recognition issue, refugee politics (aid dependence, TTA era), 'strategic depth' critique, post-2021 Taliban engagement dilemma (TTP sanctuaries vs humanitarian & trade interests).</li></ul>"},
{t:"4. Exam technique for 9389",b:"<ul><li>Essay architecture (25-mark questions): thesis upfront → 4–5 argued factors each with dated evidence & named sources (Simla 1972, NFC 2010, 18th Amendment, Indus Waters Treaty 1960) → counter-perspective honestly weighed → committed verdict ('on balance, political centralisation contributed more to 1971 than economics because…').</li><li>Source-based items: evaluate provenance (who/when/why produced), cross-reference with own knowledge, detect bias & omission; use the source as EVIDENCE in an argument, not description.</li><li>Data questions: quote figures with units & years; calculate % changes; turn data into arguments ('exports stagnating at $30bn for a decade while imports hit $80bn shows the structural deficit').</li><li>Current-affairs anchoring (2015–present): CPEC phases, 18th Amendment centre-province debates, FATF exit 2022, IMF programmes 2019/2023, population results 2023, climate disasters 2022 — named events elevate answers.</li><li>Presentation: intro defining terms (democracy, federalism, structural adjustment), paragraphs with mini-conclusions, a final judgement — write for a marker skimming at speed.</li></ul>"}]
});
